GMAIL APPLIES ONE <style> BLOCK AND DISCARDS ANY BLOCK OVER ~16 KB. Silently,
and the whole block, so the email arrives as unstyled text.

    2026-08-25 code   style 15,864   under   Aug 26 email rendered correctly
    2026-08-27 fixes  style 17,851   OVER    first email built on it was the
                                             first one that arrived unstyled

The Aug 27 digest went out on pre-merge code and was fine; everything after
PR #5 was over the line. The delivered Aug 26 mail carries exactly 15,864
characters of CSS, the broken ones 18,073.

Three earlier theories were wrong and are recorded in HANDOFF_11 so nobody
re-runs them. Body size: the broken email's body was SMALLER than the working
one's, 84 KB against 92 KB. Attachments: two probes with byte-identical HTML,
one with three PDFs and one with none, were both unstyled. Link corruption:
not real, an artefact of Gmail's API plaintext view; the hrefs are intact.

Splitting into two blocks does not work either -- tried, and Gmail applied only
the first: Crystal, Lorena, Mike and Debbie kept their colours (.cA .cB .cC .cE
in block one) while Coral, Sarahi and Amanda lost theirs (.cJ .cK .cL in block
two). That is what identified the mechanism.

prune_css drops rules whose class selectors appear nowhere in the delivered
document -- 27 rules, 2,276 bytes on 08-28 -- leaving one block at 15,797. It
runs per audience after overflow has shed its panels, so it is self-adjusting:
a panel moved to PDF takes its rules with it, and the sheet shrinks exactly
when the body grows. Only class selectors are eligible; element selectors, ids
and @media blocks are always kept, and the parser is brace-aware because a flat
regex splits inside @media and loses the wrapper. A hard guard refuses the send
if the pruned sheet is still over the cap rather than shipping unstyled mail.

GMAIL_CLIP_BYTES returns to 102,400. It was dropped to 55,000 chasing the body
-size theory and was making the report shed panels it did not need to.

Also restores the SEND_HOLD guard, which was lost in transit: it was committed
to main locally before the review branch was cut, so `git format-patch
main..HEAD` excluded it and PR #6 landed the removal of the SEND_HOLD file
without the check that reads it.

